From d6b703a87f99998d289c22545b9eb801d657d4e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iam <iam@iashur.internal>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 07:45:19 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] d/wmr: Fix AND/OR bug in the bounded controller-status wait

The 3s bounded wait added in the earlier 'Bound the controller status
wait' patch used '&&' where it needed '||': the loop condition was
'while (!have_left && !have_right && before_deadline)', which by De
Morgan's law exits the instant EITHER flag becomes true, not when BOTH
are true. On the G2's shared HID channel one controller (observed:
always left) reliably answers status requests before the other, so the
loop always returned right after the first reply -- the second
controller's status had no chance to arrive within its own bounded
wait and it stayed unregistered for the whole session, even though it
was powered on the whole time and its status did arrive shortly after.

Fix: wait for BOTH flags (or the deadline), not just one.

Reproduced 9/9 times before the fix on real hardware (HP Reverb G2);
both controllers register correctly after.
---
 src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_hmd.c | 13 +++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_hmd.c b/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_hmd.c
index f944081e0..222c16f42 100644
--- a/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_hmd.c
+++ b/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_hmd.c
@@ -2085,9 +2085,18 @@ wmr_hmd_create(enum wmr_headset_type hmd_type,
 			/* Signalled from the reader thread. Bounded: if the status reply is
 			 * lost (flaky companion USB), an unbounded wait hung startup forever
 			 * here - the reader thread now re-requests status periodically, so
-			 * missing controllers still appear later. */
+			 * missing controllers still appear later.
+			 *
+			 * Bug found 2026-08-07 (T051/T066): this was '&&' (AND) instead of
+			 * '||' (OR), so the loop exited the instant EITHER controller's
+			 * status arrived instead of waiting for BOTH - on this shared HID
+			 * channel one controller (observed: always left) reliably answers
+			 * first, and the loop would return before the second one's status
+			 * had a chance to arrive, leaving it permanently unregistered for
+			 * the session (reproduced 9/9 times). Now waits for both, still
+			 * bounded by the same 3s deadline. */
 			const int64_t deadline_ns = os_monotonic_get_ns() + (int64_t)3 * U_TIME_1S_IN_NS;
-			while (!wh->have_left_controller_status && !wh->have_right_controller_status &&
+			while ((!wh->have_left_controller_status || !wh->have_right_controller_status) &&
 			       os_monotonic_get_ns() < deadline_ns) {
 				os_mutex_unlock(&wh->controller_status_lock);
 				os_nanosleep(U_TIME_1MS_IN_NS * 20);
-- 
